# Cluster Node Management (Ansible + SOPS)

## Re-adding or Provisioning a Worker Node
Use these steps if a Raspberry Pi's SD card is wiped or if you are adding a fresh node to the cluster without touching the existing, healthy nodes.

### 1. Target Node Prerequisites (The Pi Side)
Before running Ansible, ensure the target Raspberry Pi has:
* A static IP matching the inventory file (configured via router/DHCP reservation).
* Your SSH public key copied over (`ssh-copy-id pi@<node-ip>`).
* **Cgroups Enabled:** If your playbook doesn't handle it, append the following to `/boot/firmware/cmdline.txt` (all on one single line) and reboot:
    ```text
    cgroup_enable=cpuset cgroup_enable=memory cgroup_memory=1
    ```

### 2. Update the SOPS Encrypted Inventory
If the `k3s_version` needs updating to match the live cluster, or if IPs changed, open the encrypted inventory:
```bash
sops inventory.yml

# Install Metallb
helm repo add metallb https://metallb.github.io/metallb

helm install metallb metallb/metallb --namespace metallb-system --create-namespace

# Install Nginx
helm repo add ingress-nginx https://kubernetes.github.io/ingress-nginx
helm install ingress-nginx ingress-nginx/ingress-nginx --namespace ingress-nginx --create-namespace

# Install CoreDns
# Note: The deployment.podLabels must match the service selector labels
helm install coredns coredns/coredns \
  --namespace=kube-system \
  --set service.clusterIP=10.43.0.10 \
  --set service.name=kube-dns \
  --set isClusterService=true \
  --set serviceType=ClusterIP \
  --set prometheus.service.enabled=true \
  --set kubernetes.clusterDomain=cluster.local \
  --set "deployment.podLabels.app\.kubernetes\.io/instance=coredns" \
  --set "deployment.podLabels.app\.kubernetes\.io/name=coredns" \
  --set "deployment.podLabels.k8s-app=kube-dns"